proposals.what:
SERVER: new server/next.py queue(learner, tz, limit=6) returns [{key, kind, title, reason, minutes, pts, drill (a §2.6 body) | href, urgent}]. The first match wins, in this order:
(1) a running timed test with time left: Resume;
(2) a class assignment due within 24 h;
(3) today's unfinished plan tasks, in plan order (plan.today_summary);
(4) check-ins due today (R9 pool 'checks', 3 q each), with the reason 'Fixed 10 days ago: checking it stuck';
(5) confident errors (proposal 6), then open mistakes ranked by points on the table (proposal 3);
(6) the coach's weakest skill;
(7) a Strong skill untouched for 10+ days (keep_warm);
(8) untried types in the skill worth the most points.
Today §3a calls this function instead of keeping its own first-match rule, and the palette's Suggested group reads it too, so there is one source. /api/shell.next carries the first 3 items.

SIDEBAR: a 72px 'UP NEXT' block directly under Search and Quick. It holds:
- the title (600 14.5), e.g. 'Check-in: diameter vs radius';
- a DM Mono meta, '3 q · 4 min · ≈6 pts';
- a 13px --soft reason line;
- a filled orange Start pill;
- a '›' that opens a popover with the next 5 items, each with Start and 'Not today' (snoozed until tomorrow in localStorage satf.next.snooze, keyed by item key).
Rail: a 44px orange play button whose tooltip carries the title and reason.

KEY: N starts item 1 on any shell page. Page scope wins, so on Words N stays 'add a word'.

PHONE: item 1 is the first row of the menu sheet, and the Today tab gets a dot when item 1 is urgent (a due check-in, an assignment due, or a running test).

CHAINING: the practice summary (S4), the report page (S6) and the quick sheet's close each make 'Next: {title}' their primary, read from the same queue. A student can go check-in → mistake → weak skill without going back to a menu.

STUDENT DOES: never decides what to study; presses N.
MOVES THE SCORE BECAUSE: self-directed students over-practise the skills they like and skip rechecks. This order puts spaced check-ins and the most expensive mistakes first, so spacing and error correction actually happen, and it removes the minutes of choosing that often end a session before it starts.

SERVER: new server/habits.py with about 11 families, each {id, name, tell, regex, overrides}:
- answered a different quantity than asked;
- gave the whole (or the rest) instead of the part;
- flipped a sign or direction;
- inverted a ratio, rate or slope;
- squares and roots;
- percent handling;
- units and conversions;
- mixed up two formulas (radius/diameter, area/perimeter, 180/360);
- read the wrong row, axis or point;
- stopped a step early or went a step too far;
- arithmetic slip.
The families are tagged at startup over blueprint.survey's reasons per template. A sample survey (8 seeds per template) found 2,251 distinct reasons: 827 'instead of / gave the' reasons across all 19 topics, 159 whole-vs-part across 18, 134 sign across 13, and 46 inversions across 12. The overrides dict fixes misfiles. `python3 server/habits.py` asserts that every family spans ≥ 3 topics and prints the reasons left untagged. A trap's or a wrong attempt's habit is computed from its reason, so the schema doesn't change.

ME: a 'Your habits' card after 'What keeps catching you' shows the top 3 habits by misses in the last 60 days. Each row has:
- the name (600 15);
- '11 misses across 5 skills', with skill chips;
- the trend, last 30 days vs the 30 before ('↓ 4 fewer');
- the one-sentence tell ('Before you pick, reread the last line: what exactly is asked?');
- 'Drill this habit · 8'.

DRILL: POST /api/drill {templates: the templates that have a reason in the family, the student's missed ones first, ≤ 120; want: {template: family_regex}; count 8; order 'mixed'; source 'habit'}. Every question has an option built on that habit, across different skills, interleaved. The student practises spotting the trap, not a topic.

ELSEWHERE:
- A wrong answer's verdict (S4, F3) carries a chip 'Habit: answered a different quantity' that links to the card.
- Up next adds a habit drill when one habit is ≥ 25% of the last 20 misses.
- The share view shows habits to a tutor.

STUDENT DOES: learns one check ('reread what is asked') and rehearses it across contexts, instead of fixing 11 separate mistakes on 11 question types.
MOVES THE SCORE BECAUSE: one habit costs points in every domain. Interleaved practice at telling traps apart across skills transfers better than repeating one type in a block, and on test day the tell is one check the student can run on any question.

THE SHEET: on the first shell page load of the student's week (prefs.week_start), a sheet 'Your week' opens. It can be dismissed and reopened from Me.

LAST WEEK, as receipts:
- questions answered;
- minutes against the plan;
- mistakes fixed;
- check-ins passed and relapsed;
- skills that moved up a word ('Circles: Getting there → Strong');
- the habit that fell the most;
- the estimate change, if the student sat a test.

THIS WEEK: pick up to 3 goals from 5 suggestions built from the data, each with a why and 'about N min'. For example:
- 'Fix the 4 open Circles mistakes (≈ 12 pts)'
- 'Pass all 6 check-ins due this week'
- 'Get Probability to Getting there (about 25 more answers)'
- 'Take a module on Saturday'
- 'Keep answered-a-different-quantity to 2 misses or fewer'
A custom goal can be made from the same kinds, with a number.

DATA: a new table week_goals(learner, week, id, kind, params JSON, target, created, done_at). Progress is computed on the server from attempts, traps and sessions, and /api/shell.goals returns [{title, have, target, done}].

SHOWN IN:
- the sidebar Today card, as 'Week goals 1 / 3' with pips;
- a 'This week' card at the top of Me's right column;
- the share view, for a parent;
- a toast when a goal completes.
New achievement: weeks with every goal met, 1 / 4 / 12.

STUDENT DOES: every Monday, sees last week's evidence and commits to 1 to 3 specific, checkable goals.
MOVES THE SCORE BECAUSE: specific short-term goals and tracking progress against them reliably increase follow-through (the goal-setting and progress-monitoring research). The review tells the student which effort actually moved skills.

(A) CALENDAR, ships first. The subscribed feed (R18b, served live from plan.py) titles each study-day event from the plan and the queue, e.g. 'SAT Math · 3 check-ins + Circles · 25 min'. Its DESCRIPTION lists the due items and a link to /?next=1, which starts Up next. Future days use the plan's forecast; today uses the live queue.

(B) PUSH, on the installed app, opt-in. Settings › Study gets 'Remind me on this device'. It needs:
- a service worker, web/sw.js, handling push and notificationclick only, with no caching;
- VAPID keys in env (SATF_VAPID_*);
- a table push_subs(learner, endpoint, keys JSON, created);
- a 5-minute asyncio loop that sends at study_time − remind, only on study days that aren't done yet.
The message reads '3 check-ins due · about 4 min', and a tap opens /?next=1. At most one a day, with a Stop action.

STUDENT DOES: comes back on the day the check-ins are due, not whenever they remember.
MOVES THE SCORE BECAUSE: spacing only works if the due day is met. A specific, small, time-stated nudge ('4 min') beats a generic alarm.

parity_gaps.upgrade:
- A 'Switch student' section for family devices:
  - up to 4 learners remembered on the device (satf.learners: token, name, avatar), each row with today's streak;
  - one tap switches (UI.useLearner and a reload), with a toast 'Now Maya';
  - 'Add another student' starts a fresh anonymous learner;
  - hidden on shared-computer sign-ins.
- Theme (Light/Dark) and text size (A/A+) toggles inline in the menu header.

Each row gets one action only a generated bank can offer:
- practice, quick and assignment rows: 'Again · new numbers' (same templates and tiers, fresh seeds);
- mistake rows: 'Twin';
- challenge rows: 'Re-run as practice with new numbers'.
